MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: multipart/mixed; boundary="ptf-boundary-aigen-9k"

This is a Potentia Transmission Format (PTF) document combining the closest-note finder and image generator into one movable widget.
--ptf-boundary-aigen-9k
Content-Type: text/javascript; charset=utf-8
Content-Disposition: attachment; filename="fluid.demos.noteImageGenerator"
Content-Description: layer

fluid.def("fluid.demos.noteImageGenerator", {
    $layers: "fluid.topLevelWidget",
    preferredSize: {w: 360, h: 640},
    // The closest sticky note to the widget, always on screen so its text can be checked as the widget moves
    noteFinder: {
        $component: {
            $layers: "fluid.demos.closestNoteFinder"
        }
    },
    // The image search widget: its prompt input may still be refined by typing
    imageGenerator: {
        $component: {
            $layers: "fluid.demos.imageGenerator"
        }
    },
    nearestDistance: "{noteFinder}.closest.distance",
    noNote: "$compute:fluid.demos.noteImageGenerator.hasNoNote({noteFinder}.closest.distance)",
    findNoteImage: {
        $method: {
            func: "fluid.demos.noteImageGenerator.takeNoteImage",
            args: ["{imageGenerator}", "{noteFinder}.closest.text", "{noteFinder}.closest.distance"]
        }
    },
    view: {
        $component: {
            $layers: "fluid.templateViewComponent",
            template: `<div class="note-imagegen" style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:12px; padding:12px; box-sizing:border-box; background:#f0f4c3;">
    <div style="flex:0 0 auto;">
        <button style="width:100%; padding:10px 14px; font-size:14px; border:none; border-radius:6px; background:#4a90d9; color:#fff; cursor:pointer"
                @onclick="{self}.findNoteImage()" disabled="@{noNote}">📸 Generate from closest note</button>
    </div>
    <div style="flex:0 0 190px;" @id="noteFinder"></div>
    <div style="flex:1 1 auto; min-height:0;" @id="imageGenerator"></div>
</div>`
        }
    }
});

/**
 * True when the widget is not near any sticky note, so its generate button stays disabled
 */
fluid.demos.noteImageGenerator.hasNoNote = distance => distance === null;

/**
 * Photograph the sticky note closest to the widget: use its text as the image search prompt and
 * start the search, so that the whole result appears as a single undoable step
 */
fluid.demos.noteImageGenerator.takeNoteImage = function (imageGenerator, noteText, distance) {
    if (distance === null) {
        imageGenerator.state.error = "No sticky notes nearby - move this widget closer to a note";
        return undefined;
    }
    imageGenerator.state.prompt = noteText;
    return imageGenerator.generate();
};
--ptf-boundary-aigen-9k
Content-Type: multipart/mixed; boundary="ptf-boundary-aigen-9k-inner"
Content-Disposition: attachment; filename="shape:note-imagegen"
Content-Description: component

--ptf-boundary-aigen-9k-inner
Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8
Content-Disposition: attachment; filename="/"
Content-Description: potentia

{
    "$layers": ["fluid.demos.noteImageGenerator"],
    "view": {
        "$component": {
            "shapeId": "shape:note-imagegen"
        }
    }
}
--ptf-boundary-aigen-9k-inner
Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8
Content-Disposition: attachment; filename="shape"
Content-Description: shape

{"x": 100, "y": 580, "w": 360, "h": 640}
--ptf-boundary-aigen-9k-inner--
--ptf-boundary-aigen-9k--

reasoning:
The user wants to combine the two selected widgets: closestNoteFinder (shape:finder) and imageGenerator (shape:imagegen). They want to move the combined widget close to a sticky note and press a button to generate an image.

So the combined widget should:
1. Find the closest sticky note (from closestNoteFinder)
2. When a button is pressed, take that note's text as the prompt and generate an image (from imageGenerator)

The strategy hints suggest the "dominant widget + hidden supplier" pattern. The imageGenerator is dominant (shows the result the user wants to see and act on), and the closestNoteFinder supplies a prompt (the closest note's text).

So I should define a fresh layer extending fluid.demos.imageGenerator, with fluid.topLevelWidget as the last layer, and mix in closestNoteFinder as a hidden subcomponent.

Wait, let me think about the wiring. The imageGenerator has:
- state.prompt (input the user types)
- generate() method which uses state.prompt

The closestNoteFinder computes `closest.text` which is the text of the closest note.

The wiring: when the user presses a button, take the closest note's text and generate an image using it as the prompt.

Options:
1. A button that calls a method which reads the note text and kicks off generation. This would be a new method in the fresh layer: `generateFromNote`.
2. Wire state.prompt to the note text - but then the user wouldn't be able to type their own prompt... Actually, the user might want both.

The user's direction: "so I can move it close to a sticky note and press a button to generate an image". So the combined widget:
- Moves on canvas (it does, as a widget)
- Finds the closest sticky note
- Has a button which, when pressed, generates an image from that note's text

The economical pattern from the hint:

```js
fluid.def("my.ai.showsFoundResult", {
    $layers: ["my.resultShower", "fluid.topLevelWidget"],
    finder: {
        $component: {
            $layers: "my.textFinder",
            view: "{showsFoundResult}.view"
        }
    },
    state: {
        query: "{finder}.foundText"
    }
});
```

But that wires the supplier's output to the dominant widget's input continuously. Here we want the button press to trigger generation from the latest note text. Hmm.

Actually, maybe both approaches can work. If I wire `state.prompt` to `{finder}.closest.text`, then the prompt shows the closest note's text, and clicking "Find Image" generates an image of that note. But that would make the text input display the note text - though the user can still type (live-state binding writes back to state.prompt, overwriting the reference?). Actually no - if state.prompt holds a reference `{closestNoteFinder}.closest.text`, then writing to it writes to the referenced site, not the component. Hmm, that's how sited references work. Per the docs: "A reference to a path holding mutable configuration is sited: writing through it writes to the original site."

So if I set `state: {prompt: "{closestNoteFinder}.closest.text"}`, typing in the input would write to closestNoteFinder.closest.text, which is `{self}.closest.text`, which is `{self}.closest.text` - a computed from findClosest, not writable. So that would break.

Better: add a new method `generateFromNote` that takes the closest text and runs the search with it. But the generate method sets `state.prompt` then searches... Actually the generate function reads state.prompt. I could just write:

```js
generateFromNote: {
    $method: {
        func: (self, state, noteText) => {
            state.prompt = noteText;
            return fluid.demos.imageGenerator.generate(state);
        },
        args: ["self", ...]
    }
}
```

Hmm, but `func` args for $method: without `args`, the function is called with the component followed by caller's args: `func(that, ...args)`.

Let me design the fresh layer:

```js
fluid.def("fluid.demos.nearbyImageGenerator", {
    $layers: ["fluid.demos.imageGenerator", "fluid.topLevelWidget"],
    preferredSize: {w: 360, h: 520},
    noteFinder: {
        $component: {
            $layers: "fluid.demos.closestNoteFinder",
            view: "{nearbyImageGenerator}.view"
        }
    },
    findNoteImage: {
        $method: {
            func: "fluid.demos.nearbyImageGenerator.findNoteImage",
            args: ["{self}.state", "{noteFinder}.closest.text"]
        }
    }
});

fluid.demos.nearbyImageGenerator.findNoteImage = function (state, prompt) {
    state.prompt = prompt;
    return fluid.demos.imageGenerator.generate(state);
};
```

Wait - but there's a subtlety. The template of imageGenerator includes an input bound to state.prompt. If I display the note text in the input... The user could also just type. Hmm.

Should the template show the closest note text? The imageGenerator template doesn't have a place for it. I could add a subcomponent showing the closest note (like the noteFinder's own view does) as part of the combined widget's view. But the noteFinder's view is hidden (set to the imageGenerator's view).

Hmm wait - the hint says the supplier's view set to the fresh widget's view, so the supplier doesn't render anything. But the supplier itself may have subcomponents... The noteFinder has a `distanceLine` subcomponent with its own view. If noteFinder's view is redirected to the main view, then noteFinder's template doesn't render... but distanceLine's view is its own subcomponent - it would still render? Actually no - distanceLine is a subcomponent of noteFinder, and it renders as part of noteFinder's view template at `@id="distanceLine"`. If noteFinder's view is redirected to the umbrella's view, then noteFinder's template never renders, so distanceLine never appears. The hint confirms: "The supplier then has no view of its own, so neither it nor any of its subcomponents renders anything".

Hmm, but wait - actually there's an issue with using `view: "{nearbyImageGenerator}.view"` as a reference in the $component record. The `$layers` key of the $component record: referencing a layer name or component by context name resolves "a component bearing that layer, in scope". So `view: "{nearbyImageGenerator}.view"` - context `nearbyImageGenerator` is the layer name's nickname, and the component with that layer in scope is the umbrella itself. Then `.view` gets its view member. That's the documented pattern from the hint.

Actually wait, let me be careful about the context name resolution. `{nearbyImageGenerator}` - the nickname of layer `fluid.demos.nearbyImageGenerator` is `nearbyImageGenerator`. The search: "A layer name or its nickname (last segment), e.g. `{my.app.panel}` or `{panel}` - A component bearing that layer, in scope". The umbrella component bears that layer, and the subcomponent noteFinder is a child of the umbrella, so the umbrella is in its parent's scope. Yes, that resolves.

Also "A member name beats a layer name at the same level" - in noteFinder's scope, `nearestImageGenerator` hmm no conflict here.

Now the template: I need to add a button "Generate" (or "Find note image"?) and show the closest note text. Modifying the template of the imageGenerator - the fresh layer inherits template from the parent layer. Can a fresh layer override the template? Yes, by redefining `view.$component.template`... Actually the template lives in the view subcomponent. Hmm.

Alternative approach: add a subcomponent that displays the closest note and a button within the combined widget. But embedding requires the template to have @id elements.

Option A: Override the whole template in the fresh layer. That duplicates a lot of the imagegen template. Not ideal but workable.

Option B: Add a new subcomponent `noteInfo` in the fresh layer with its own view (templateViewComponent), and inject it into the DOM near the image generator. But subcomponents without an @id in the template won't render anywhere. Unless the template of imageGenerator has an @id container... it doesn't.

Hmm, but I could add the button + note display as a separate subcomponent, and modify the imageGenerator's template? No - don't modify existing layers... Actually the instructions say "Only redefine an existing layer when the user asks for every widget made from it to change." I could add to the existing imageGenerator layer... no wait, the workspace's layers are defined by me via PTF. Actually I CAN define new layers; I shouldn't redefine existing ones (fluid.demos.imageGenerator) since that would change the existing imageGenerator widget.

Actually, hold on. The replacement of widgets: the user selected shape:finder and shape:imagegen. The combined widget should replace them? "Combine the functionality of the two selected widgets" - I think the user wants a single combined widget on the canvas replacing the two, so they can move it around. I'll create one fresh widget from the new layer, and delete the two old ones. Hmm - should I? The hint says "leave the widgets it was composed from in place unless the user asks for them to be replaced." The user asked to "combine the functionality", which suggests making a new combined widget. Deleting the originals might be presumptuous, but having four widgets (two original, one combined, plus moving) might be clutter. The user said "so I can move it close to a sticky note and press a button to generate an image" - the combined widget will be movable. The originals would be redundant.

Hmm. I think the safest interpretation: create a fresh combined widget, and delete the two selected originals? Or leave them? The direction says "Combine the functionality of the two selected widgets so I can..." - The purpose is a new widget with that functionality. I'll create the fresh one and remove the two selected originals to avoid duplication - actually wait, deleting loses the user's state (imagegen might have state). The originals were just instantiated from layers, no live state shown (no live parts). So deleting them loses nothing.

Hmm, but "leave the widgets it was composed from in place unless the user asks for them to be replaced" - the hint explicitly says to leave them unless asked. The user didn't explicitly say "replace them". But combining implies replacing with one widget? To be safe I could leave the originals and just add the combined widget. But that leaves clutter: two widgets become three. The user said "Combine the functionality of the two selected widgets" - I read this as: make a new widget that does both things. They didn't say "replace". I'll create the combined widget and delete the two originals? Hmm...

Actually, let me reconsider: the instructions say "Make the smallest change which achieves the user's direction." Deleting the originals isn't needed for the widget to work. Leaving them is the default. But the user's intent seems to be to have one combined widget they can drag around. Having duplicate widgets that overlap could be confusing but harmless. The strategy hint explicitly advises leaving them in place unless asked to replace. I'll follow the hint and leave them in place. Hmm, but then the user sees three widgets. The combined one will sit near their selection (below the selected shapes, based on strategy hints: "A fresh widget without one is placed beside the user's selection").

Hmm, actually, let me reconsider once more. "Combine the functionality of the two selected widgets so I can move it close to a sticky note and press a button to generate an image." The emphasis: one movable widget. I think creating the combined widget is the core. I'll leave the originals.

Now, template design for the combined widget. I want:

- Show the closest sticky note's text (like noteFinder does) — nice to display which note will be imaged.
- A button to generate an image from that note.
- Keep the prompt input so user can refine/typing their own prompt too.

The imageGenerator's template shows a text input bound to state.prompt. If state.prompt is only set when the user presses the new button, the input stays user-controlled otherwise. That works nicely:

- New method `findNoteImage()` reads `{noteFinder}.closest.text`, sets `state.prompt` to it, and calls generate.
- Wait, but if no notes exist, closest.text is "No sticky notes found" - then generating with that prompt would produce a weird image or an error "No images...". Hmm. findClosest returns text "No sticky notes found" with distance null when there are no notes. If the user presses the button while not near a note, it'd search for "No sticky notes found". Not ideal but acceptable; or I could guard: if no notes, set error instead.

Let me make the helper:

```js
fluid.demos.nearbyImageGenerator.fromNote = function (self, noteText, hasNote) {
    const state = self.state;
    if (!hasNote) {
        state.error = "Move near a sticky note first";
        return undefined;
    }
    state.prompt = noteText;
    return fluid.demos.imageGenerator.generate(state);
};
```

And method:

```js
findNoteImage: {
    $method: {
        func: "fluid.demos.nearbyImageGenerator.fromNote",
        args: ["{self}", "{noteFinder}.closest.text", "{noteFinder}.closest.distance"]
    }
},
```

distance null ⇒ no notes. Or check text? distance is null ⇔ no notes found (when bounds exist) — in findClosest, distance is null when there are no bounds or no notes. Good.

Template: I need a button and a display of the nearest note. The simplest is to override the whole template in the fresh layer. Since the fresh layer extends imageGenerator, I define `view` configuration... The SFC loader adds `$layers` and view. My fresh layer will be a text/javascript layer (not an SFC). A JS layer needs to supply its own view subcomponent with a template, or inherit.

Wait — fluid.demos.imageGenerator is an SFC: its script defines the layer without explicit `view` subcomponent but the loader adds `view` with `fluid.sfcTemplateViewComponent` and the template. If my fresh JS layer extends it, the view is inherited from the parent layer (subcomponent records propagate by merging). Can I override the view's template? Yes:

```js
view: {
    $component: {
        $layers: ["fluid.sfcTemplateViewComponent", "<my own template>"]
    }
}
```

Hmm, the `$layers` in `$component` adds layers to the existing view subcomponent (subcomponent precedence - the fresh view config merges with inherited one). But the template member: imageGenerator's view has `templateLayer` member (fluid.sfcTemplateViewComponent uses `templateLayer`)... The sfcTemplateViewComponent's template comes from the SFC's template block keyed by... The loader adds `$layers: ["fluid.component", "fluid.sfcLayer"]` and a view subcomponent with layer fluid.sfcTemplateViewComponent "whose template is the SFC's `<template>` block". Presumably via `template` member or `templateLayer`. From the docs: `fluid.sfcTemplateViewComponent` — "The `<template>` block of the SFC named by `templateLayer` (added by the loader)". So the view has `templateLayer: "fluid.demos.imageGenerator"` maybe, and sfcTemplateViewComponent resolves the SFC. If I override with `template: "..."`... a `template` member from a weaker `subcomponent` layer vs `templateLayer`... if I set `template`, and the view still has templateLayer, conflict. Hmm, this could get messy.

Alternatively, define the combined widget as a text/x-vue layer that extends fluid.demos.imageGenerator:

```vue
<script>
fluid.def("fluid.demos.noteImageGenerator", {
    $layers: ["fluid.demos.imageGenerator", "fluid.topLevelWidget"],
    preferredSize: {w: 360, h: 540},
    noteFinder: {...},
    findNoteImage: {...}
});
</script>

<template>... inherits? </template>
```

Wait — SFC loader adds `fluid.sfcTemplateViewComponent` whose template is "the SFC's template block". If the fresh SFC has its own template, the view would use the fresh template, and NOT the parent's template? The view's `templateLayer` would point to the fresh layer name. Hmm, so my fresh SFC's `<template>` would replace the parent's template entirely - I'd have to include the whole imagegen UI again plus the note bits. That's duplication.

Hmm. Is there a way to combine templates? Maybe I can extend the parent layer's template string with $compute? That's awkward.

Alternative: don't override the template. Keep the imageGenerator's exact UI, and the combined widget just adds:
1. The noteFinder subcomponent (hidden view, computes closest text).
2. The `findNoteImage` method.
3. A small extra UI? Without modifying the template, where does the button go?

Hmm. Option: give the noteFinder's view its own sub-templating... no, noteFinder's view is hidden (redirected).

Wait, actually, maybe I should reconsider. What if the noteFinder remains VISIBLE (not hidden), and the combined widget is an umbrella with its own template containing both? The hint describes the dominant+hidden pattern as "most economical", but an umbrella with both visible is also legitimate: "If both widgets must remain visible, the umbrella may instead have a template of its own holding an @id element for each subcomponent."

But do both widgets need to remain visible? The imageGenerator's UI is the image result; the noteFinder shows closest note text. The user's goal: move near a note, press a button, get an image. Displaying which is the closest note is useful feedback. Two widgets stacked: e.g., the top part shows "Closest Sticky Note: <text>" with a "Generate" button, and below everything the image generator UI with its prompt input, Find Image, Another buttons.

Using the hint's own compositional example, with the note text wired into state.prompt via the button action... but the button needs to live somewhere visible.

Approach 1: Umbrella layer with own template:

```js
fluid.def("fluid.demos.noteImageGenerator", {
    $layers: "fluid.topLevelWidget",
    preferredSize: {w: 360, h: 560},
    noteFinder: {
        $component: {
            $layers: "fluid.demos.closestNoteFinder"
        }
    },
    imageGenerator: {
        $component: {
            $layers: "fluid.demos.imageGenerator",
            state: {prompt: "{noteFinder}.closest.text"}?? 
        }
    },
    view: {
        $templateViewComponent?...
```

Hmm wait, but wiring `state.prompt: "{noteFinder}.closest.text"` — again sitting/writing issues. The input writes back to state.prompt which would then write to the closest text site (unwritable computed). The docs say: "Read-only sources. If the site holds a computed value ($compute), it is not written. The attribute is re-materialised from the element's state, so the page still serialises faithfully." Oh nice — live-state write-back to a read-only (computed) source is not written; the attribute is re-materialised from the element's state. So `value="@{prompt}"` where prompt is a computed value from the other component: typing writes... hmm wait, actually let me re-read: "If the site holds a computed value ($compute), it is not written." OK so the write is suppressed. But then it's not two-way - typing wouldn't update the prompt used for generation. Hmm, but the user's flow: move near note, press generate. They might also want to refine the prompt by typing. 

With the button approach: button sets state.prompt = note text; then generate uses it. And typing can refine it before pressing "Find Image" or the "Generate from note" button. I think the button action setting state.prompt is the clean flow, keeping input two-way.

So maybe: display note text in a visible noteFinder subcomponent? But noteFinder's whole template is the note display widget.

Umbrella with own template: the umbrella has two subcomponents, each with their own view (noteFinder visible, imageGenerator with its own view). For the umbrella to host both, its view template needs @id elements: `@id="noteFinder"` and `@id="imageGenerator"`. Per docs: "`view`: \"{other.component}.view\"... A subcomponent with its own template... If the second should share the view..." Also: "In an SFC... The element is watched live... `@id=\"member\"` marks the element as the container for the subcomponent at member". A subcomponent with its own view (like fluid.demos.* via SFC) renders its template INTO the @id element's container? The @id element's contents are used only if subcomponent is fluid.selfTemplateViewComponent; otherwise the subcomponent renders its own template into that container.

Hmm, how does an umbrella with two SFC-based subcomponents lay out? fluid.viewComponent has `elideParent` default true (template root merged with container). Each subcomponent's view renders into its own container (the @id element). So the umbrella's template would be:

```html
<div class="note-imagegen">
    <div class="@id noteFinder"?>
```

Wait, `@id` goes on the container element: `<div @id="noteFinder"></div>` and `<div @id="imageGenerator"></div>`, and the subcomponents render their own templates inside. elideParent merges the widget template root into the container div — a plain div root inside the container, nested. That yields a stack: note finder box on top, image generator below.

The umbrella must be an SFC (to have a template) or a JS layer with explicit view template:

```js
fluid.def("fluid.demos.noteImageGenerator", {
    $layers: "fluid.topLevelWidget",
    preferredSize: {w: 360, h: 560},
    noteFinder: {
        $component: {
            $layers: "fluid.demos.closestNoteFinder"
        }
    },
    imageGenerator: {
        $component: {
            $layers: "fluid.demos.imageGenerator"
        }
    },
    view: {
        $component: {
            $layers: "fluid.templateViewComponent",
            template: `
                <div class="note-imagegen">
                    <div @id="noteFinder"></div>
                    <div @id="imageGenerator"></div>
                </div>`
        }
    }
});
```

This is a text/javascript layer — allowed (fluid.def with view, as in the docs example my.greeting). The container passed/forwarded: creator function with container - for a JS layer with templateViewComponent, container forwarded... The docs: "A container passed to a creator function is forwarded here." Yes.

But hmm, wait: do I even need to define the imageGenerator as a subcomponent, or can I just show the note text inside it? The imageGenerator template's notefinder display... Let me think about what layout is most useful and least code:

Combined widget shows:
1. Title? "Image Generator" already in imagegen.
2. Closest note text + Generate button — this is the new functionality.
3. The imagegen UI (prompt input, Find Image, Another, result image).

The umbrella approach duplicates views but composes cleanly without touching existing layers. The wiring: findNoteImage button... where does its handler live? I can add the button to the umbrella's template and have it call `{noteFinder}.???`... The button handler syntax: `@onclick="{self}.findNoteImage()"` — method on the umbrella, which reads `{noteFinder}.closest.text` and calls `{imageGenerator}.state.prompt = ...; {imageGenerator}.generate()`. Writes through references: `that.imageGenerator.state.prompt = x` - is that allowed via template? `@onclick` holds a method invocation or assignment. The method `findNoteImage` on the umbrella can do it in JS: writing through the component proxy into a subcomponent's live layer works (that.imageGenerator.state...). But via a $method, args resolved as references — `{imageGenerator}` resolves to the subcomponent (context name = member name). Passing the subcomponent proxy as an arg: the $method func receives it. Then `func(noteFinder, imageGenerator)`: imageGenerator is a proxy — can I call `.generate()` on it? Yes, proxy reads/methods work. And write `state.prompt = x` via proxy write — "Writes through the component proxy into its live layer" - state.root is reactiveRoot: writing to state.prompt through proxy writes to live layer at that path? The docs: "Writes (that.count = 6, or mutating array methods such as that.items.push(x)) are recorded in the component's live layer... A write beneath a $reactiveRoot is applied immutably to the root's value." So `imageGenerator.state.prompt = "fox"` would set state.prompt in its live layer. And calling `imageGenerator.generate()` - a $method - works through proxy.

So:

```js
findNoteImage: {
    $method: {
        func: "fluid.demos.noteImageGenerator.findNoteImage",
        args: ["{noteFinder}", "{imageGenerator}"]
    }
}
```

with

```js
fluid.demos.noteImageGenerator.findNoteImage = function (noteFinder, imageGenerator) {
    const text = noteFinder.closest && noteFinder.closest.text;
    const distance = noteFinder.closest && noteFinder.closest.distance;
    if (distance === null) {
        imageGenerator.state.error = "No sticky notes nearby";
        return undefined;
    }
    imageGenerator.state.prompt = text;
    return imageGenerator.generate();
};
```

Hmm wait, but also: what about keeping the note display? noteFinder's template: title "Closest Sticky Note", text. That covers "move close to a sticky note" feedback: the text shown updates as it moves near notes. 

And the button in the umbrella template between them:

```html
<div class="note-imagegen">
    <div class="note-imagegen-head">
        <button @onclick="{self}.findNoteImage()">📸 Generate from note</button>
    </div>
    <div @id="noteFinder"></div>
    <div @id="imageGenerator"></div>
</div>
```

Hmm wait, noteFinder's elideParent: it renders a div.notefinder as merged with the @id container. The @id container... Let me look at how subcomponents render into @id elements. From the docs, `@id="todoItems"` — "Its contents are taken as the subcomponent's template unless the subcomponent supplies its own." fluid.selfTemplateViewComponent uses markup inside the element as template; a subcomponent with its own view (like noteFinder's SFC view) renders its own template into the element, replacing contents.

The fluid.viewComponent `container` is that @id element; `elideParent: true` merges the vTree root with the container — the rendered root element becomes the container itself? For tldraw-hosted widgets, the container is the shape element; elideParent merges template root with container. So the notefinder div merges with its @id div. Fine.

